<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>Kneepoint report — {{ meta.target }}</title>
<style>
  :root { --ink:#111; --muted:#667; --ok:#0a7d33; --warn:#b26b00; --bad:#b3261e; }
  body { font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui; color: var(--ink); max-width: 960px;
         margin: 2rem auto; padding: 0 1rem; }
  h1 { margin-bottom: .2rem; } .muted { color: var(--muted); font-size: .9rem; }
  .verdict { font-size: 1.25rem; margin: 1rem 0; padding: .8rem 1rem;
             background: #f4f6f8; border-left: 4px solid var(--ink); border-radius: 4px; }
  .cards { display: flex; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 1rem 0; }
  .card { flex: 1 1 200px; border: 1px solid #dde; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem; }
  .card .big { font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 700; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 1rem 0; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: .5rem .75rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #dde; }
  .pass { color: var(--ok); font-weight: 600; } .degraded { color: var(--warn); font-weight: 600; }
  .fail { color: var(--bad); font-weight: 600; }
  .placeholder { color: var(--muted); font-style: italic; padding: 1rem;
                 border: 1px dashed #ccd; border-radius: 8px; }
  .contaminated { margin: 1rem 0; padding: .8rem 1rem; background: #fff5e6;
                  border-left: 4px solid var(--warn); border-radius: 4px; }
  .howto { color: var(--muted); font-size: .9rem; margin: .6rem 0 1.4rem;
           padding-left: .75rem; border-left: 3px solid #dde; }
  .howto a { color: var(--muted); }
</style>
{#- "How to read this" — one line per section, worded from the chapter of the
    Book that owns the metric and linking to it. The report is a teaching
    surface for someone who has never used Kneepoint; the Book is the source,
    so a sentence that is not in the Book does not belong here either. -#}
{% macro howto(slug, title) -%}
<p class="howto"><strong>How to read this.</strong> {{ caller() }}
<a href="https://docs.kneepoint.dev/book/{{ slug }}/">Book: {{ title }} →</a></p>
{%- endmacro %}
</head>
<body>
<h1>Kneepoint report</h1>
<p class="muted">{{ meta.target }} · model {{ meta.model }} · {{ meta.started }} ·
ramp {{ meta.ramp }} · chaos {{ meta.chaos }} ·
{{ meta.total_sessions }} sessions / {{ meta.total_requests }} requests</p>

<div class="verdict">
{% if knee_summary %}<strong>{{ knee_summary.headline }}</strong>
{% elif knee %}Knee point: <strong>{{ knee.concurrency }} concurrent sessions</strong>
  <span class="muted">({{ knee.method }})</span>
{% else %}No clear knee found in this ramp.{% endif %}
</div>

{% if knee_summary %}
<table>
  <tr><th>Method</th><th>Knee</th><th>Note</th></tr>
  {% for verdict in (knee_summary.kneedle, knee_summary.p95_doubling) %}
  <tr><td><code>{{ verdict.method }}</code></td>
      <td>{% if verdict.concurrency is not none %}{{ verdict.concurrency }}{% else %}none{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="muted">{{ verdict.note or "" }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
{% if knee_summary.ratios %}
<p class="muted">p95 against the curve floor, which is what the doubling
heuristic decides on — a value marked * is within 15% of the 2.0 threshold and
flips between repetitions on run noise alone:<br>
{% for r in knee_summary.ratios %}c={{ r.concurrency }}
{{ "%.2f"|format(r.ratio) }}×{% if r.near_threshold %}*{% endif %}{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}
</p>
{% endif %}
{% for note in knee_summary.notes %}
<p class="muted">{{ note }}.</p>
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% call howto("throughput", "Throughput & capacity") %}The knee is where
degradation accelerates, not a limit you must never exceed: below it, adding load
costs a little latency; above it, a lot. When a range is given, <strong>plan for
the low end</strong> — being under the knee costs some headroom, being over it
costs everyone's latency at once. The practical operating point is usually a
level or two below, chosen against your SLO. p95 doubling is the headline and
can be checked by hand from the JSONL; Kneedle corroborates, and when the two
disagree the disagreement lowers the confidence rather than replacing the
answer. A knee is not meaningful without quality — read it next to the
resolution rate below.{% endcall %}

{% if contaminated %}
<div class="contaminated">
<strong>Contaminated levels: {{ contaminated | map(attribute="concurrency") | join(", ") }}.</strong>
{{ abandoned_total }} request{{ "" if abandoned_total == 1 else "s" }} were abandoned at the
client wall. Kneepoint closes the connection the moment it gives up, but a server is
free to finish generating anyway — so these levels may be measuring work left over
from the level before, and are no longer independent. Treat their latencies, and any
knee that depends on them, as unpublishable. Every chart below shades the ramp from
the first contaminated level onward.
</div>
{% endif %}

<h2>The knee curve</h2>
{{ knee_div | safe }}
{% call howto("foundations", "Foundations") %}Read p50 and p95 together and ask
where p95 bends. p95 is the signal: the median stays flat long after the system
starts struggling, and p99 is too noisy at these sample sizes to trend. Before
you read the shape, read the <code>n</code> under each level — a curve that
spikes where n=8 is an absence of data, not a spike, and a level below
{{ min_samples }} requests is marked <em>not plotted</em> rather than drawn from
thin air. A gap in a line is a suppressed point, not a rendering bug; y starts at
zero and nothing is smoothed. The dashed line is the low end of the knee range.
A slowdown can also be the model writing more: check output tokens before
blaming the server.{% endcall %}

<h2>Quality under load</h2>
{% if drift_div %}{{ drift_div | safe }}
<p class="muted">Latency rising is a capacity problem you can solve with more
machines. Resolution <em>falling</em> is a different problem: the agent is
answering worse, not just slower, and no amount of hardware fixes a wrong
answer. Both axes start at a real zero and neither line is smoothed — every
point is a measured level, and the counts behind it (requests for p95, judged
sessions for the rate) sit under each level on the axis. A level below
{{ min_samples }} of either is marked there and left off its trace.</p>
{% call howto("quality", "Quality under load") %}Everything lost between the
first level and a higher one is degradation <em>caused by load</em> — timeouts
landing mid-reasoning, retry storms, tool latency stacking. When resolution
bends at the same level as latency, or <strong>before</strong> it, this one
chart is the whole argument for capacity-testing agents. Drift needs
repetitions too: a single-run drift curve is not evidence, because the same
configuration can fall steeply in one repetition and stay flat in the next.{% endcall %}
{% else %}<p class="placeholder">{{ quality_thin_note or quality_note or "Resolution not measured — configure a resolution check." }}</p>{% endif %}

<h2>The quality curve</h2>
{% if quality_div %}{{ quality_div | safe }}
{% call howto("quality", "Quality under load") %}Resolution is the share of
<em>judged</em> sessions in which the agent actually accomplished the task.
Sessions with no verdict are left out of the denominator, never counted as
failures, and the judged count sits under each level. Read the lowest level as
the ceiling — the model's best score with no queueing and no pressure. A
baseline of 100% falling to 78% under load means the system broke the agent; a
baseline of 75% falling to 72% means the model never followed the format, and
the fix is the prompt, not the infrastructure. Resolution says whether the task
completed; it does not score factual accuracy, tone or safety — that is evals,
a different discipline.{% endcall %}
{% else %}<p class="placeholder">{{ quality_thin_note or quality_note or "Resolution not measured — configure a resolution check." }}</p>{% endif %}

<h2>Time to first token vs. time per output token</h2>
{% if ttft_div %}{{ ttft_div | safe }}
<p class="muted">Two different slow-downs on one chart. <strong>TTFT</strong> rising
means requests are queueing before the model starts — a capacity problem that
more replicas fix. <strong>TPOT</strong> rising means generation itself is
contended once it has started — decode batching or the model's own throughput.
They are different units on different axes, both from zero; hover for the sample
count behind each point.</p>
{% call howto("latency", "Latency metrics") %}This single comparison is the most
useful diagnostic in the Book. <strong>TTFT rising, TPOT flat</strong>: queueing —
requests wait to start but generate at full speed once started; the server is at
its concurrency limit, and more replicas help. <strong>TPOT rising, TTFT
flat</strong>: decode saturation — memory bandwidth or KV-cache pressure from many
simultaneous streams; a faster-memory machine helps, more concurrency will not.
<strong>Both rising</strong>: past the knee, out of headroom everywhere.
<strong>Neither, yet total latency is up</strong>: not the model — look at tool
calls and retries. On a reasoning-capable model TTFT includes thinking time, so
a chart built on it is a chart of reasoning length unless the reasoning knob was
off; record which.{% endcall %}
{% else %}<p class="placeholder">No TTFT or TPOT to chart — the target produced no streamed content, or no level reached {{ min_samples }} requests.</p>{% endif %}

<h2>Inter-token latency</h2>
{% if itl_div %}{{ itl_div | safe }}
<p class="muted">The gap between streamed chunks: the median, and the worst pause
a user met at each level (the p99 of every request's own p99). Per chunk, not per
token — see the note under the breakdown table.</p>
{% call howto("latency", "Latency metrics") %}TPOT is an average and hides
stutter: a stream that delivers evenly every 40 ms and one that alternates 5 ms
and 75 ms have the same TPOT and feel completely different — the p99 is where
the stutter shows. Read it against TPOT above. Inter-chunk gap <em>flat</em>
while TPOT climbs is a fixed flush interval hiding a slowing decoder; gaps that
<em>stretch</em> mean the stream really is stalling. Only when the chunk count
is about the output token count is this inter-token latency in the strict
sense.{% endcall %}
{% else %}<p class="placeholder">No inter-token latency recorded — the target returned each answer in a single chunk, so there was no gap to measure.</p>{% endif %}

<h2>Latency breakdown</h2>
{% if latency_rows %}
<table>
  <tr><th>Concurrency</th><th>n</th><th>TTFT p50</th><th>TTFT p95</th>
      <th>TPOT p50</th><th>ITL p50</th><th>ITL p99</th></tr>
  {% for s in latency_rows %}
  <tr><td>{{ s.concurrency }}</td><td>{{ s.n }}</td>
      <td>{{ "%.0f ms"|format(s.ttft_p50_ms) }}</td>
      <td>{{ "%.0f ms"|format(s.ttft_p95_ms) }}</td>
      <td>{% if s.tpot_p50_ms is not none %}{{ "%.2f ms/tok"|format(s.tpot_p50_ms) }}{% else %}<span class="muted">n/a</span>{% endif %}</td>
      <td>{% if s.itl_p50_ms is not none %}{{ "%.2f ms"|format(s.itl_p50_ms) }}{% else %}<span class="muted">n/a</span>{% endif %}</td>
      <td>{% if s.itl_p99_ms is not none %}{{ "%.2f ms"|format(s.itl_p99_ms) }}{% else %}<span class="muted">n/a</span>{% endif %}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
<p class="muted"><strong>ITL is measured per streamed chunk, not per token</strong>
— a server decides how many tokens go in a chunk, so on some servers this is the
stream's flush cadence rather than decode speed. TPOT is per reported token.
<code>n/a</code> means the provider never reported the underlying field; it is
never a zero. See the
<a href="https://docs.kneepoint.dev/output-format/">output format reference</a>.</p>
{% call howto("latency", "Latency metrics") %}Which column answers which
question: <em>does the UI feel responsive?</em> — TTFT p95, the pause the user
stares at. <em>Does the text stream smoothly?</em> — ITL p99 for stutter, TPOT
for decode speed. <em>Is the server queueing or decoding slowly?</em> — TTFT
against TPOT, the chart above. Total latency is the sum of these parts, and
they fail for different reasons — telling them apart is most of
diagnosis.{% endcall %}
{% else %}<p class="placeholder">No TTFT recorded — the target produced no streamed content.</p>{% endif %}

<h2>Errors</h2>
{% if error_levels %}
{% if total_failed == 0 %}
<p class="muted">No request failed at any level — every one of the
{{ error_levels | sum(attribute="requests") }} requests returned a successful response.</p>
{% else %}
<p class="muted">{{ total_failed }} of {{ error_levels | sum(attribute="requests") }}
requests failed. Errors first appear at concurrency {{ first_error.concurrency }}{% if first_error.concurrency == error_levels[0].concurrency %}
— <strong>present from the first level</strong>, which points at configuration
rather than capacity{% else %} — absent below it, which is a capacity signal{% endif %}.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Concurrency</th><th>Requests</th><th>Failed</th><th>Error rate</th>
      <th>Status codes</th><th>Most common errors</th></tr>
  {% for lv in error_levels %}
  <tr><td>{{ lv.concurrency }}</td><td>{{ lv.requests }}</td><td>{{ lv.failed }}</td>
      <td{% if lv.error_rate >= 0.05 %} class="fail"{% elif lv.failed %} class="degraded"{% endif %}>{{ "%.1f"|format(lv.error_rate * 100) }}%</td>
      <td>{% for sc in lv.status_codes %}<code>{{ sc.status }}</code>&nbsp;×{{ sc.count }}{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% else %}<span class="muted">—</span>{% endfor %}</td>
      <td>{% for e in lv.top_errors %}{{ e.error }} <span class="muted">×{{ e.count }}</span>{% if not loop.last %}<br>{% endif %}{% else %}<span class="muted">—</span>{% endfor %}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
<p class="muted">A <code>429</code> is the provider throttling you, not struggling;
a <code>5xx</code> is the server genuinely failing; <code>no code</code> is a
timeout or a dropped connection — the client gave up before any status arrived.
These have different fixes, so never read the rate without the mix.
{% set abandoned_failures = error_levels | sum(attribute="abandoned") %}
{% if abandoned_failures %}{{ abandoned_failures }} of the failures
{{ "was" if abandoned_failures == 1 else "were" }} Kneepoint abandoning a request at
its own wall; see the contamination banner above.{% endif %}</p>
{% endif %}
{% call howto("stability", "Stability & resilience") %}What matters is the
<em>shape</em>, not the number: errors that appear only above a certain
concurrency are a capacity signal; errors present from the first level are a
configuration bug. A healthy system under increasing load degrades in a known
order — the tail stretches, p95 bends, retries appear, errors appear,
resolution falls. When you see that sequence you are watching normal
saturation. When the order is different — resolution falling while latency and
errors stay flat — something more interesting is happening: context truncation,
quality collapse, or a judge problem.{% endcall %}
{% else %}<p class="placeholder">No request records to analyse.</p>{% endif %}

<h2>Retry amplification</h2>
{% if retry_levels %}
<table>
  <tr><th>Concurrency</th><th>Sessions</th><th>Turns asked for</th>
      <th>Requests issued</th><th>Retries</th><th>Amplification</th></tr>
  {% for lv in retry_levels %}
  <tr><td>{{ lv.concurrency }}</td><td>{{ lv.sessions }}</td>
      <td>{{ lv.first_attempts }}</td><td>{{ lv.requests }}</td>
      <td>{{ lv.retries }}</td>
      <td{% if lv.amplification >= 1.2 %} class="degraded"{% endif %}>{{ "%.2f"|format(lv.amplification) }}×</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
{% if worst_retry %}
<div class="contaminated">
<strong>The generator added load exactly where the target was struggling.</strong>
At concurrency {{ worst_retry.concurrency }} it issued {{ worst_retry.requests }}
requests for {{ worst_retry.first_attempts }} turns — {{ "%.2f"|format(worst_retry.amplification) }}×.
Retrying a failed turn is what a real client does, so this is the honest default,
but it means a ramp can partly manufacture the knee it measures. Disclose it when
you publish this curve, or lower <code>conversation.retry.max_attempts</code> in
the scenario and say that instead.
</div>
{% else %}
<p class="muted">No retries fired: every turn succeeded first time, so the
requests above are exactly the workload the ramp asked for.</p>
{% endif %}
{% call howto("stability", "Stability & resilience") %}Retries are how systems
hide instability from users — and how they burn budget invisibly. A retry costs
full price in tokens and capacity while producing no additional user value, and
it adds load to an already-loaded system: the classic feedback loop that turns a
slowdown into an outage. Amplification rising with concurrency means your users
may not see errors, but you are paying for them — the bill is the retry-waste
card under Cost.{% endcall %}
{% else %}<p class="placeholder">No request records to analyse.</p>{% endif %}

<h2>Cost</h2>
{% if cost %}
<div class="cards">
  <div class="card"><div class="muted">$ / resolved task</div>
    <div class="big">{% if cost.cost_per_resolved is not none %}${{ "%.4f"|format(cost.cost_per_resolved) }}{% else %}n/a{% endif %}</div>
    <div class="muted">{{ cost.resolved_sessions }} of {{ cost.judged_sessions }} judged sessions resolved</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="muted">Total spend</div>
    <div class="big">${{ "%.4f"|format(cost.total_spend) }}</div>
    <div class="muted">{{ cost.sessions }} sessions</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="muted">Retry waste</div>
    <div class="big">{{ "%.1f"|format(cost.retry_waste_pct * 100) }}%</div>
    <div class="muted">${{ "%.4f"|format(cost.waste_spend) }} on failed attempts</div></div>
</div>
{% call howto("cost", "Cost & token economics") %}$/resolved task prices the
outcome, not the activity: cost per request rewards a system that fails cheaply,
and an agent that burns 3,000 tokens and gives up looks great by it while the
user got nothing. Expect this figure to rise as load approaches the knee —
retries multiply spend without producing resolutions and a falling resolution
rate shrinks the denominator — so there is often a <strong>cost knee that
arrives before the latency knee</strong>. If the target is a local model, these
are a projection at the configured rates, not spend; always say so. Before
trusting any of it: are the rates the ones you are billed at, and were token
counts reported rather than null? A null count is unknown, never zero.{% endcall %}
{% else %}<p class="placeholder">{{ cost_note or "Cost not measured (set --price-in / --price-out or a cost block in the scenario)." }}</p>{% endif %}

<h2>Resilience</h2>
{% if res %}
<div class="cards">
  <div class="card"><div class="muted">Resilience score</div>
    <div class="big">{% if res.score is not none %}{{ "%.0f"|format(res.score) }}{% else %}n/a{% endif %}</div>
    <div class="muted">{{ res.faulted_sessions }} faulted vs {{ res.clean_sessions }} clean sessions</div></div>
</div>
<table>
  <tr><th>Fault</th><th>Sessions hit</th><th>Resolved</th><th>Verdict</th></tr>
  {% for row in res.rows %}
  <tr><td><code>{{ row.fault }}</code></td><td>{{ row.sessions_hit }}</td>
      <td>{% if row.resolved_rate is not none %}{{ "%.0f"|format(row.resolved_rate * 100) }}%{% else %}too few samples{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="{{ row.verdict or '' }}">{{ row.verdict or "n/a" }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
{% call howto("stability", "Stability & resilience") %}The score is the
resolution rate of sessions that hit a fault as a percentage of the rate for
sessions that did not. <strong>100 means the faults made no difference</strong>
— the agent absorbed them; a low score means injected failures translate
directly into failed user tasks. The grid is where the actionable detail lives:
an agent can handle rate limits perfectly because its HTTP client retries them,
and fail completely on malformed tool output because nobody wrote that error
path. <em>pass</em> is at least 95% of the clean rate, <em>degraded</em> at
least 70%, <em>fail</em> below that; <em>too few samples</em> is a fault seen by
fewer sessions than the run's <code>min_group</code> and is not a pass.{% endcall %}
{% else %}<p class="placeholder">{{ res_note or "Resilience not measured (run with --chaos standard)." }}</p>{% endif %}

<h2>Run metadata</h2>
{% if metadata_rows %}
<table>
  {% for label, value in metadata_rows %}
  <tr><th style="width: 9rem">{{ label }}</th><td>{{ value }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
<p class="muted">Read from the run's <code>-meta.json</code> sidecar, which is
what <code>kneepoint report</code> re-renders from. A figure from this report is
only comparable to another run's if the ramp, hold, seed and chaos profile here
match; quote them alongside the knee.</p>
{% call howto("foundations", "Foundations") %}Two of these rows decide how much
to trust everything above. <strong>The hold</strong> (in the Ramp row): the
first seconds of each level are warm-up — cold caches, filling connection pools — and not
representative; a 5–20 s hold is fine for a smoke test but folds that noise into
the numbers, so benchmarks use far longer. <strong>Platform</strong>: if the
load generator and the model share a machine they compete for CPU and memory
bandwidth, and latency at high concurrency is inflated relative to a remote
generator — realistic for a laptop, unrealistic for production; state which.
And one run is an anecdote: publish or decide on three repetitions, reporting
the median and the spread, never the best.{% endcall %}
{% else %}<p class="placeholder">{{ meta_note or "Run metadata not recorded — this report was rendered without the run's metadata sidecar, so hold, seed, versions and thresholds are unknown." }}</p>{% endif %}

<p class="muted">Generated by <a href="https://kneepoint.dev">kneepoint</a> — find where your AI agent breaks.</p>
</body>
</html>
